Problem summary

The platform reports inconsistent Python ML availability: backend sometimes shows Python service down even when the Python Flask server is running, and analyses often use only a subset of available Python ML models (e.g., BERTopic is missing on many systems). We need a robust fix that: 1) reliably reports Python service and per-model availability to the Node backend and API consumers, 2) adds a background health poll + cache to avoid flapping and expensive sync health checks, 3) provides per-analysis metadata about which Python models were actually used (and which failed), 4) provides clear docs for enabling optional models like BERTopic and how to verify the system, and 5) adds a compatibility wrapper so index.js health-checks are consistent with existing code.

Scope of changes (actionable tasks)

1) Add a new health cache service to backend: backend/services/healthCache.js
   - Polls Python /health periodically (configurable interval and timeout via env vars)
   - Caches status, available_models, lastChecked, lastSuccessful, and error
   - Exposes start/stop and getCachedPythonStatus functions
   - Automatically starts polling when imported

2) Update Python NLP client: backend/services/pythonNLPClient.js
   - Add checkPythonML compatibility wrapper that returns {status, available_models, lastChecked}
   - Ensure default export exposes checkPythonML
   - Improve runCompletePythonPipeline to return structured metadata fields: models_attempted, models_used, models_failed (if the Python response contains available_models or provenance, use that; otherwise infer)
   - Keep existing functions but ensure better structured return values for transparency

3) Update backend index.js health endpoint to use the cached status from healthCache instead of ad-hoc requests
   - Include lastChecked/lastSuccessful/available_models in /api/health response

4) Add documentation file: docs/api/HEALTH_CHECK.md
   - Explain polling/cache behavior, env vars, troubleshooting steps, manual verification (curl), and BERTopic installation notes

5) Append a short troubleshooting and BERTopic guidance to backend/python_services/README.md (update file by appending a section)

6) Add small logging improvements in pythonNLPClient.logPythonServiceStatus to rely on cached models if available

