Implements mode-checking for mutable references

The place pointed to by a mutable reference is always considered to be an 'exec-mode' place, even when the reference itself is in proof-mode. As we discussed before, this is necessary to allow the coercion of an 'exec-mode &mut T' to a 'tracked &mut T'. This also means there is a restriction that a mutable borrow can only occur at an exec-mode place.

Proof-mode and spec-mode places are created by: tracked/ghost locals, tracked/ghost fields, and the Tracked/Ghost types. Specifically, Tracked and Ghost "contain" a proof-mode place and spec-mode place respectively. This place can be accessed using the new `PlaceX::ModeUnwrap` (which is created from the `Tracked::borrow_mut` and `Ghost::borrow_mut`).

Example: If you have `x: &mut u64`, you can only modify the value from executable code, since `*x` is an exec-mode place.

But if you have `&mut Tracked<X>` or `&mut Ghost<X>` you can modify the value from inside proof code, because these values contain ghostly places:

```rust
let r: &mut Tracked<X> = ...;
proof {
    // r    -> exec-mode value
    // *r   -> exec-mode place of type Tracked<X>
    // *r.borrow_mut()   -> proof-mode place of type X

    // So you can do this:
    *r.borrow_mut() = X { ... };
}
``` 

Utilities to make this system easy to use will come later.

Note that some of the mode-checking code being modified by this PR is currently used even outside the new-mut-ref experiment. A couple error messages changed, but I think they are mostly improvements.

